values ('bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000006', 'en', null, 'Ancient Rome: History & Civilization', 'A guided tour of Rome from the legendary founding in 753 BCE to the fall of the West in 476 CE, covering kingdom, republic, empire, society, engineering, and lasting legacy. The structure and factual backbone are adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia (CC BY-SA 4.0). All prose here is original, with dates given in BCE and CE.', null, 'foundations', 46, true, 'humanities', '{}')
on conflict (id) do nothing;

insert into public.lessons (course_id, position, title, kind, body, grammar_notes_md, estimated_minutes)
select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000006', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much Rome do you already know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"By tradition, the city of Rome was founded in which year?","options":["509 BCE","753 BCE","27 BCE","476 CE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Roman tradition dated the founding to 753 BCE, credited to the legendary Romulus — see ''The founding and the Roman Kingdom''."},{"prompt":"After the monarchy was overthrown in 509 BCE, Rome became a…","options":["kingdom ruled by one hereditary king","republic led by elected magistrates","direct democracy of all its residents","permanent military dictatorship"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Republic replaced kings with annually elected officials checked by the Senate and assemblies."},{"prompt":"Rome''s great rival in the three Punic Wars was the city of…","options":["Athens","Carthage","Alexandria","Babylon"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carthage, a Phoenician power in North Africa, fought Rome from 264 to 146 BCE."},{"prompt":"Who is generally regarded as Rome''s first emperor?","options":["Julius Caesar","Augustus","Nero","Constantine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Octavian took the name Augustus in 27 BCE and founded the Principate; Caesar was dictator, never emperor."},{"prompt":"Julius Caesar was assassinated in which year?","options":["49 BCE","44 BCE","27 BCE","14 CE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"He was killed on the Ides of March (15 March) 44 BCE by a group of senators."},{"prompt":"Which religion spread across the empire and eventually became its official faith?","options":["Islam","Christianity","Buddhism","Zoroastrianism"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Christianity grew from the first century CE and became the state religion under Theodosius in 380 CE."},{"prompt":"The Western Roman Empire is traditionally said to have ended in which year?","options":["330 CE","410 CE","476 CE","1453 CE"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In 476 CE the last western emperor, Romulus Augustulus, was deposed by Odoacer."},{"prompt":"Which Roman engineering structure was built mainly to carry water into cities?","options":["the ziggurat","the aqueduct","the triumphal arch","the basilica"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aqueducts used a gentle downhill gradient to deliver fresh water over long distances — see ''Roman engineering and architecture''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The founding and the Roman Kingdom', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to place the founding of Rome on a timeline, separate legend from history, and explain how and why the early monarchy was replaced. Warm-up from the diagnostic: which came first, the traditional founding of the city or the start of the Republic, and roughly how many years apart were they? Keep that gap in mind, because the whole of Rome''s early story unfolds inside it."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Roman tradition placed the founding of the city in 753 BCE and credited it to Romulus, the surviving twin of the she-wolf legend. Modern historians treat that date as symbolic; villages on the hills above the Tiber had merged into a town over the preceding centuries. For its first roughly two and a half centuries Rome was a kingdom. Tradition names seven kings, beginning with Romulus and ending with Tarquinius Superbus, Tarquin the Proud. The later kings were heavily influenced by the neighboring Etruscans, who shaped Roman religion, engineering, and the symbols of power. Kingship was not strictly hereditary; a king was chosen and then confirmed. Around 509 BCE the Romans expelled Tarquin and swore never again to accept a king, replacing one-man rule with the Republic."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"753 BCE: traditional founding of Rome, attributed to Romulus, the first of the seven legendary kings. Numa Pompilius, the second king, was remembered for organizing Roman religion and the calendar. The fifth and seventh kings belonged to the Etruscan Tarquin family, under whom Rome grew into a real city with drained marshland, a forum, and large temples. 509 BCE: Lucius Junius Brutus led the revolt that drove out Tarquinius Superbus after the outrage against the noblewoman Lucretia, ending the monarchy. The hatred of kingship born here lasted for centuries and later made the word king, rex, a dangerous accusation in Roman politics. The Etruscan legacy endured in Roman dress, divination, and the arch."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what year does tradition place the founding of Rome? 2) Who was the legendary first king? 3) Which neighboring people strongly shaped the later monarchy? 4) Who was the seventh and last king? 5) In roughly what year did the monarchy end? Answers: 1) 753 BCE. 2) Romulus. 3) The Etruscans. 4) Tarquinius Superbus, Tarquin the Proud. 5) About 509 BCE, when he was expelled and the Republic began."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the she-wolf and the twins as sober history rather than foundation legend. Mistake: assuming the seven kings are an exact, documented list; the early regal period is reconstructed from later tradition. Mistake: forgetting there is no year zero, so counting across the BCE/CE line is off by one if you include it. Exit ticket: (a) Was the 753 BCE founding date recorded at the time or assigned by later tradition? (b) What strong political feeling did the expulsion of Tarquin leave behind? Answers: it was assigned by later Roman tradition; a lasting hatred of monarchy and of anyone who seemed to want to be king."}]}'::jsonb, 'Dates before the year 1 count downward: 753 BCE is earlier than 509 BCE. There is no year zero between BCE and CE.', 13),
  (3, 'The Roman Republic: Senate, consuls, and assemblies', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to name the main institutions of the Roman Republic and explain how they checked one another. Warm-up from the diagnostic: the Republic replaced one-man rule with elected officials, but who or what kept any one of those officials from becoming a new king? As you read, watch for three answers: short terms, shared offices, and the power of veto."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Republic, lasting from about 509 to 27 BCE, divided power so that no single person could dominate. At its head stood two consuls, elected for one year, each able to veto the other. Below them ran a ladder of offices, the cursus honorum: quaestor, aedile, praetor, and consul, with the censorship above. The Senate, a standing council of several hundred former magistrates, controlled finance and foreign policy through its immense prestige and advice. Popular assemblies passed laws, elected officials, and decided war and peace. After the long Conflict of the Orders between patricians and plebeians, the plebeians won their own officials, the tribunes, who were sacrosanct and could veto almost any act. In a grave emergency the Romans could appoint a single dictator, but only for six months."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"Around 494 BCE the plebeians, in a dispute with the patrician elite, won the office of tribune of the plebs, able to block harmful measures with the cry of veto, ''I forbid.'' About 451 to 450 BCE Rome published the Twelve Tables, its first written law code, so that the rules no longer lived only in patrician memory. The Senate, though formally only advisory, guided the state for centuries because consuls served a single year while senators served for life. The consulship itself was shared by two men precisely so that each could restrain the other. Together the formula SPQR, the Senate and the People of Rome, captured the balance the system aimed for."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many consuls were there at a time, and for how long did they serve? 2) What was the standing council of former magistrates called? 3) What power let one consul, or a tribune, block an act? 4) Which officials protected the interests of the plebeians? 5) For how long could an emergency dictator legally hold power? Answers: 1) Two consuls, each for one year. 2) The Senate. 3) The veto. 4) The tribunes of the plebs. 5) Six months."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining the Senate passed laws like a modern parliament; formally it advised, while assemblies legislated, yet its prestige made that advice decisive. Mistake: confusing the republican dictator, a temporary emergency office capped at six months, with the later permanent one-man rule of Caesar. Mistake: thinking all Romans could vote equally; voting was structured by class and weighted against the poor. Exit ticket: (a) What did a tribune mean by shouting veto? (b) Why did two consuls share the top office? Answers: ''I forbid,'' blocking the act in question; so that each could check and restrain the other."}]}'::jsonb, 'The Latin phrase SPQR stands for Senatus Populusque Romanus, ''the Senate and the People of Rome'' — the two halves of republican authority.', 14),
  (4, 'The Punic Wars and Mediterranean expansion', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to list the three Punic Wars in order, give their rough dates, and explain how they turned Rome from an Italian power into the master of the Mediterranean. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you already know Rome''s great rival was Carthage. As you read, ask a sharper question: what did Rome actually gain, territory and attitude alike, from three generations of war against a single enemy?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Carthage, a wealthy trading city in North Africa, was Rome''s equal when the two collided over Sicily. The First Punic War, 264 to 241 BCE, was fought largely at sea and ended with Rome seizing Sicily as its first overseas province. The Second Punic War, 218 to 201 BCE, was the most dangerous: the Carthaginian general Hannibal marched an army, elephants included, over the Alps into Italy and crushed Roman forces, most famously at Cannae in 216 BCE. Yet Rome refused to surrender, carried the war to Africa, and defeated Hannibal at Zama in 202 BCE. The Third Punic War, 149 to 146 BCE, ended with Carthage besieged, captured, and destroyed. In the same year Rome also sacked Corinth, announcing its dominance over Greece."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"264 to 241 BCE: the First Punic War, won at sea, gives Rome Sicily. 218 BCE: Hannibal crosses the Alps into Italy. 216 BCE: at Cannae he destroys a far larger Roman army, one of history''s classic encirclements, yet cannot take the city of Rome itself. 202 BCE: the Roman general Scipio, later called Africanus, defeats Hannibal at Zama in North Africa, ending the Second Punic War. 146 BCE: in the Third Punic War, Rome razes Carthage; the same year it destroys Corinth. These victories left Rome without a rival in the western Mediterranean and flooded Italy with wealth, enslaved captives, and new provinces to govern."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What were the rough dates of the First Punic War, and what did Rome gain? 2) Which general led Carthage in the Second Punic War? 3) At which 216 BCE battle did Rome suffer a famous disaster? 4) Where and in what year was Hannibal finally defeated? 5) What happened to Carthage in 146 BCE? Answers: 1) 264 to 241 BCE; Rome gained Sicily, its first province. 2) Hannibal. 3) Cannae. 4) At Zama in 202 BCE, by Scipio Africanus. 5) It was captured and destroyed, ending the wars."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Hannibal lost because he could never win a battle; he won repeatedly in Italy but could not break Rome''s web of alliances or take the city. Mistake: merging the three wars into one; they span more than a century, 264 to 146 BCE. Mistake: assuming victory brought only glory; the flood of enslaved people and cheap grain strained Italian society and set up the next century''s crises. Exit ticket: (a) How many Punic Wars were there? (b) What was Rome''s first overseas province? Answers: three; Sicily."}]}'::jsonb, 'The word ''Punic'' comes from the Latin for Phoenician, because Carthage was founded by Phoenician settlers from the eastern Mediterranean.', 14),
  (5, 'The crisis and fall of the Republic', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to trace, in order, the men and conflicts that broke the Republic: the Gracchi, Marius and Sulla, and finally Caesar. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you know Caesar was killed in 44 BCE. As you read, build toward a harder question: why did a system that had checked ambition for four centuries suddenly fail to stop a single general from seizing control?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Conquest made Rome rich but tore at its society. Small farmers were ruined while the elite built huge estates worked by enslaved people. Reformers tried to fix this and were killed for it: Tiberius Gracchus, tribune in 133 BCE, pushed land reform and was murdered, as was his brother Gaius a decade later. Next came the age of generals. Marius reshaped the army so that soldiers looked to their commander, not the state, for land and pay. His rival Sulla marched that kind of army on Rome itself and ruled as dictator. The pattern was set. Caesar, Pompey, and Crassus formed an informal alliance; after conquering Gaul, Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE, won the civil war, and made himself dictator for life, until senators killed him in 44 BCE."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"133 BCE: Tiberius Gracchus, as tribune, proposes redistributing public land to poor citizens and is beaten to death by senators; his brother Gaius renews the effort and dies in 121 BCE. From 107 BCE: Marius opens the army to the landless poor, who then depend on their general for rewards. 88 and again 82 BCE: Sulla marches on Rome and rules as dictator, murdering enemies through published lists called proscriptions. 60 BCE: the First Triumvirate of Caesar, Pompey, and Crassus. 49 BCE: Caesar crosses the Rubicon, starting civil war. 44 BCE: on the Ides of March he is assassinated as dictator for life."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which brothers sought land reform and were both killed? 2) What dangerous change did Marius make to the army? 3) Who first marched a Roman army on Rome and ruled by proscription? 4) What did Caesar do in 49 BCE that made civil war inevitable? 5) In what year, and on what date, was Caesar assassinated? Answers: 1) The Gracchi, Tiberius and Gaius. 2) He recruited landless poor who looked to their general for reward. 3) Sulla. 4) He crossed the Rubicon with his army. 5) 44 BCE, on the Ides of March, 15 March."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling Caesar the first emperor; he was dictator, and the Empire proper began only with his heir. Mistake: treating the Gracchi as marginal; their deaths showed that reform could now be answered with murder, poisoning politics for good. Mistake: blaming one villain; the deeper causes were economic ruin and armies loyal to men rather than to Rome. Exit ticket: (a) Why did army reform threaten the Republic? (b) Was Caesar an emperor or a dictator? Answers: soldiers became loyal to the general who paid and settled them, not to the state; he was a dictator, not an emperor."}]}'::jsonb, 'To ''cross the Rubicon'' still means to take an irreversible step: in 49 BCE Caesar led his army across that small river into Italy, which the law forbade.', 15),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1: kingdom, republic, and conquest', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Rome''s monarchy ended and the Republic began in roughly which year?","options":["753 BCE","509 BCE","264 BCE","27 BCE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expulsion of Tarquinius Superbus around 509 BCE opened the republican period."},{"prompt":"The standing council of former magistrates that guided the Republic was the…","options":["assembly","Senate","tribunal","consulate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Senate advised on finance and foreign policy, and its prestige made that advice decisive."},{"prompt":"The two chief magistrates of the Republic, elected each year, were the…","options":["tribunes","consuls","censors","praetors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two consuls shared the top office so each could veto the other."},{"prompt":"Hannibal destroyed a large Roman army in 216 BCE at the battle of…","options":["Zama","Cannae","Actium","the Rubicon"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cannae was a classic encirclement, yet Hannibal still could not take the city of Rome."},{"prompt":"Carthage was finally destroyed, ending the Third Punic War, in…","options":["202 BCE","149 BCE","146 BCE","44 BCE"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rome razed Carthage in 146 BCE and sacked Corinth in the same year."},{"prompt":"Tiberius Gracchus, tribune in 133 BCE, was killed while pushing for…","options":["land reform for poor citizens","the abolition of the Senate","war against Carthage","a new state religion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"His proposal to redistribute public land alarmed the elite, who had him murdered."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (7, 'Augustus and the founding of the Empire', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to explain how Octavian became Augustus, what the Principate was, and why historians count him as Rome''s first emperor rather than Caesar. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you picked Augustus as the first emperor. As you read, pin down the trickier point: how did he take total power while carefully pretending the old Republic still lived?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"After Caesar''s murder, his heir Octavian and the general Mark Antony first allied, then fought. Octavian defeated Antony and Cleopatra at the naval battle of Actium in 31 BCE, leaving himself the sole master of Rome. Rather than take the hated title of king, in 27 BCE he accepted the name Augustus and the role of princeps, ''first citizen.'' He kept the Senate, the consuls, and the forms of the Republic while holding the real power through command of the armies and lifelong authority. This disguised monarchy is called the Principate. His long reign opened the Pax Romana, roughly two centuries of relative peace and prosperity across the Mediterranean world. When Augustus died in 14 CE, power passed smoothly to his stepson, proving that the Empire would outlast its founder."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"43 BCE: Octavian, Antony, and Lepidus form the Second Triumvirate and hunt down Caesar''s assassins. 31 BCE: Octavian''s fleet defeats Antony and Cleopatra at Actium; the pair soon take their own lives, and Egypt becomes a Roman province. 27 BCE: the Senate grants Octavian the name Augustus; the Principate begins and he is reckoned the first emperor. From 27 BCE to about 180 CE: the Pax Romana, an era of comparative peace. 14 CE: Augustus dies after more than forty years in power, and his stepson Tiberius succeeds him, founding the Julio-Claudian line of emperors."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) At which 31 BCE battle did Octavian defeat Antony and Cleopatra? 2) What name and title did Octavian accept in 27 BCE? 3) What is the disguised-monarchy system he created called? 4) What long era of relative peace did his reign begin? 5) In what year did Augustus die? Answers: 1) Actium. 2) Augustus, as princeps or first citizen. 3) The Principate. 4) The Pax Romana. 5) 14 CE."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Augustus abolished the Senate; he kept it and the republican offices as a respectable front while holding real control of the army. Mistake: dating the Empire to Caesar; the imperial system begins with Augustus in 27 BCE. Mistake: picturing the Pax Romana as total peace; it meant relative internal stability, not an end to all warfare on the frontiers. Exit ticket: (a) Why did Augustus avoid the title of king? (b) What do we call the era of relative peace he began? Answers: the title rex was hated since the monarchy''s fall, so he ruled behind republican forms; the Pax Romana."}]}'::jsonb, '''Augustus'' was a title meaning ''revered'' or ''majestic'', not a birth name; it later became a standard part of an emperor''s style.', 14),
  (8, 'Roman society, daily life, and engineering', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to describe how Roman society was organized, from the family to the class system and slavery, and explain the engineering that made Roman cities possible. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you identified the aqueduct as a water-carrying structure. As you read, connect two things that are usually taught apart: the shape of Roman society and the famous feats of Roman building were deeply linked."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"At the base of Roman life was the familia, headed by the paterfamilias, who held sweeping legal power over wife, children, and property. Society was sharply ranked: senators and the wealthy equestrians at the top, ordinary free citizens below, and, beneath everyone, a vast population of enslaved people who did much of the farming, mining, and household labor. Slavery was not based on race, and freed slaves, called freedmen, could gain limited citizenship, though their former owners kept claims on them. Binding the classes together was the patron-client relationship of mutual favors. On this social base Rome built astonishing public works: stone-paved roads, arched aqueducts, and durable concrete made with volcanic ash. Grand structures like the Colosseum, opened around 80 CE, used arches and vaults to seat tens of thousands."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"Roman concrete, mixed with volcanic ash called pozzolana, could even set underwater and let builders raise domes and vaults far larger than stone alone allowed. The road network began with the Via Appia in 312 BCE and eventually linked the whole empire, aiding trade and the swift march of armies. The first aqueduct, the Aqua Appia, also dates to 312 BCE; later lines crossed valleys on towering arches. The Colosseum, formally the Flavian Amphitheatre, was begun under the emperor Vespasian and opened under his son Titus around 80 CE, holding perhaps fifty thousand spectators. The Pantheon, rebuilt under Hadrian about 126 CE, still carries the largest unreinforced concrete dome ever made."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What was the head of a Roman household called? 2) Name two groups in the Roman class system above the ordinary poor. 3) What ingredient made Roman concrete so strong and able to set underwater? 4) In what year did both the first major road and the first aqueduct begin? 5) Around what year did the Colosseum open? Answers: 1) The paterfamilias. 2) Senators and equestrians. 3) Volcanic ash, pozzolana. 4) 312 BCE. 5) About 80 CE."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming Roman slavery was based on race; people were enslaved mainly through war, debt, or birth, and could sometimes be freed. Mistake: thinking aqueducts pumped water uphill; they relied on a steady, gentle downhill gradient over long distances. Mistake: crediting the Colosseum to Augustus; it was a Flavian project opened around 80 CE. Exit ticket: (a) What legal power did the paterfamilias hold? (b) What made Roman concrete exceptional? Answers: patria potestas, wide authority over his family and property; volcanic ash that produced strong concrete able to set even underwater."}]}'::jsonb, 'The head of a Roman household was the paterfamilias, who held wide legal authority, patria potestas, over his family and property.', 15),
  (9, 'Religion, Christianity, and the later Empire', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to describe traditional Roman religion, trace the rise of Christianity from persecuted sect to state faith, and explain the third-century crisis that reshaped the later Empire. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you chose Christianity as the faith that became official. As you read, follow the longer arc: how did Rome move from many gods and emperor worship to a single Christian state religion within a few centuries?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Traditional Roman religion was polytheistic, honoring gods such as Jupiter, Juno, and Minerva with public rituals led by priests and overseen by the emperor as chief priest. Worship of the emperor himself helped bind the far-flung provinces. Christianity, spreading from the first century CE, refused this emperor worship and so was intermittently persecuted. Meanwhile the state nearly collapsed. The crisis of the third century, roughly 235 to 284 CE, brought a rapid churn of soldier-emperors, invasions, plague, and ruinous inflation, with breakaway regions in the west and east. The emperor Diocletian restored order after 284 CE by reorganizing the government and sharing rule among several colleagues. His successor Constantine then legalized Christianity, and later in the century it became the empire''s official religion."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"64 CE: the emperor Nero blames Christians for a great fire in Rome, an early persecution. 235 to 284 CE: the crisis of the third century, when dozens of emperors rise and fall amid war and economic collapse. 284 CE: Diocletian takes power and stabilizes the state, later sharing rule in a four-man system. 303 CE: Diocletian launches the last great persecution of Christians. 313 CE: Constantine and his colleague issue the Edict of Milan, granting toleration. 325 CE: the Council of Nicaea defines Christian doctrine. 380 CE: Theodosius makes Christianity the official religion of the empire."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name two chief gods of the traditional Roman state religion. 2) Why were Christians persecuted by the Roman state? 3) What roughly dates the crisis of the third century? 4) Which emperor restored order after 284 CE? 5) In what year did Christianity become the official religion? Answers: 1) Jupiter and Juno (also Minerva). 2) They refused to take part in emperor worship and the state cults. 3) About 235 to 284 CE. 4) Diocletian. 5) 380 CE, under Theodosius."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Constantine made Christianity the official religion; the Edict of Milan in 313 CE only legalized it, and Theodosius made it official in 380 CE. Mistake: picturing constant, empire-wide persecution; it was usually local and intermittent until the great persecution of 303 CE. Mistake: treating the third-century crisis as a minor rough patch; it nearly destroyed the state and forced deep reforms. Exit ticket: (a) What did the Edict of Milan of 313 CE do? (b) How did Diocletian respond to the crisis? Answers: it granted toleration to Christianity; he reorganized the government and shared rule among several colleagues to restore stability."}]}'::jsonb, 'The emperor held the title pontifex maximus, chief priest of the state religion; centuries later the same Latin title passed to the bishop of Rome, the pope.', 15),
  (10, 'The fall of the West and Rome''s lasting legacy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"You will be able to explain why the Western Roman Empire fell, note that the East carried on, and identify the main ways Rome still shapes the modern world. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you gave 476 CE as the traditional end of the Western Empire. As you read, sharpen two ideas: ''fall'' means the collapse of the western half only, and the deepest Roman legacies are things we still use every day."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"By the late fourth century the empire was permanently split into western and eastern halves. The west, poorer and more exposed, buckled under repeated invasions and migrations of Gothic, Vandal, and other peoples, along with weak government and economic strain. Rome itself was sacked in 410 CE and again in 455 CE. In 476 CE the last western emperor, the boy Romulus Augustulus, was deposed by the commander Odoacer, the date traditionally marking the end of the Western Roman Empire. The eastern half, centered on Constantinople, survived for nearly another thousand years as the Byzantine Empire. Rome''s legacy outlived its power: its law, its language, and its ideas of government still shape the world. Latin became the Romance languages, Roman law underlies many legal systems, and republican ideas inspired later governments."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"330 CE: Constantine founds Constantinople as a new eastern capital. 378 CE: Goths crush a Roman army at Adrianople. 395 CE: the empire is permanently divided between a western and an eastern emperor. 410 CE: the Visigoths under Alaric sack the city of Rome. 455 CE: the Vandals sack Rome again. 476 CE: Odoacer deposes Romulus Augustulus, the conventional end of the Western Empire. The eastern, Byzantine, Empire endured until 1453 CE. In the sixth century the eastern emperor Justinian codified Roman law in a great compilation that shaped European legal tradition for centuries."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what year is the Western Roman Empire traditionally said to fall, and who was deposed? 2) Which half of the empire survived, and under what name? 3) Name one people who sacked the city of Rome, and the year. 4) What family of modern languages descends from Latin? 5) Name one lasting Roman contribution besides language. Answers: 1) 476 CE; Romulus Augustulus was deposed by Odoacer. 2) The eastern half, as the Byzantine Empire. 3) The Visigoths in 410 CE, or the Vandals in 455 CE. 4) The Romance languages. 5) Roman law (also ideas of republican government and much architecture)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: saying ''Rome fell in 476 CE'' without qualification; only the western half ended, while the eastern empire continued to 1453 CE. Mistake: imagining a single dramatic collapse; the west declined over generations from many causes. Mistake: thinking Rome''s influence ended with the empire; its law, language, and government ideas are still with us. Exit ticket: (a) What exactly ended in 476 CE? (b) Give two enduring legacies of Rome. Answers: the Western Roman Empire, when Romulus Augustulus was deposed; any two of Roman law, the Latin-based Romance languages, and republican ideas of government."}]}'::jsonb, 'The modern ''Romance'' languages (French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, Romanian) are named for Rome because they all descend from spoken Latin.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000006' and l.position = v.pos
);
